1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,937
Attenuation is where your signal goes to die — a fraction of a dB at a time.

2
00:00:06,937 --> 00:00:12,328
It's the reduction of signal strength as energy passes through a medium, a cable, a

3
00:00:12,328 --> 00:00:17,395
connector, or an obstacle, and it's expressed in decibels because losses in dB

4
00:00:17,395 --> 00:00:19,733
simply subtract from a power budget.

5
00:00:19,733 --> 00:00:24,800
The numbers bite fast: a loss of just six dB means only a quarter of the power

6
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:25,385
survives.

7
00:00:25,835 --> 00:00:27,767
There are only two mechanisms.

8
00:00:27,767 --> 00:00:32,920
Spreading: a fixed amount of power gets diluted over a larger area, like ripples

9
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,144
widening on a pond.

10
00:00:34,144 --> 00:00:39,103
And absorption: the medium converts RF energy into heat, like a shout muffled

11
00:00:39,103 --> 00:00:40,005
inside a wall.

12
00:00:40,005 --> 00:00:45,093
Everything that eats your signal is one of those two — and because each loss is

13
00:00:45,093 --> 00:00:49,023
logged in dB, the total is just a running sum down the chain.

14
00:00:49,473 --> 00:00:52,115
Walk the losses in a real receive path.

15
00:00:52,115 --> 00:00:57,738
Cable first: coax loss rises with both frequency and length — a cheap RG-58 run can

16
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:03,090
lose several dB per ten metres at four hundred megahertz, which is why low-loss

17
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:04,987
cable and short runs matter.

18
00:01:04,987 --> 00:01:10,475
Then every connector, filter, splitter, and switch adds its own insertion loss, a

19
00:01:10,475 --> 00:01:14,269
fraction of a dB to a few dB each — and they accumulate.

20
00:01:14,269 --> 00:01:19,757
Out in the world, walls, foliage, and terrain absorb and scatter energy, and loss

21
00:01:19,757 --> 00:01:24,702
through material generally worsens at higher frequencies — the reason UHF

22
00:01:24,702 --> 00:01:27,412
penetrates buildings less well than VHF.

23
00:01:27,412 --> 00:01:32,832
And way up above ten gigahertz, the atmosphere itself joins in: oxygen and water

24
00:01:32,832 --> 00:01:38,252
vapour absorb RF and rain adds "rain fade" — though below UHF that's negligible.

25
00:01:38,702 --> 00:01:40,806
Two rules fall out of that list.

26
00:01:40,806 --> 00:01:45,804
First, the recurring theme: for most of these mechanisms, higher frequencies

27
00:01:45,804 --> 00:01:50,801
attenuate more — that single fact shapes band choice, cable choice, and site

28
00:01:50,801 --> 00:01:51,393
planning.

29
00:01:51,393 --> 00:01:56,390
Second, the sneaky one: a lossy cable ahead of the first amplifier is doubly

30
00:01:56,390 --> 00:02:01,716
harmful, because it doesn't just weaken the signal — it degrades the noise figure

31
00:02:01,716 --> 00:02:03,163
of the whole receiver.

32
00:02:03,163 --> 00:02:05,727
Loss before the first amp counts twice.

33
00:02:05,727 --> 00:02:09,804
That's why the preamp belongs at the antenna, not at the desk.

34
00:02:10,254 --> 00:02:13,374
Now the twist: attenuation is not always the enemy.

35
00:02:13,374 --> 00:02:17,901
A deliberate attenuator protects a receiver from strong signals that would

36
00:02:17,901 --> 00:02:22,917
otherwise overload the front end and drive the converter past full scale, spraying

37
00:02:22,917 --> 00:02:25,425
intermodulation products across the band.

38
00:02:25,425 --> 00:02:30,197
Trading a few dB of wanted signal for headroom against a nearby transmitter is

39
00:02:30,197 --> 00:02:35,214
often a net win — and filters attenuate out-of-band energy for the same protective

40
00:02:35,214 --> 00:02:35,642
reason.

41
00:02:36,092 --> 00:02:40,764
One scope note: free-space spreading over the air is a kind of attenuation

42
00:02:40,764 --> 00:02:46,004
important enough to have its own name — path loss — while the feedline losses we've

43
00:02:46,004 --> 00:02:49,792
just walked are the part of the budget you actually control.

44
00:02:49,792 --> 00:02:54,969
And for GopherTrunk, the arithmetic is unforgiving: it sees only what survives the

45
00:02:54,969 --> 00:02:56,169
path and the cable.

46
00:02:56,169 --> 00:03:00,967
Attenuation upstream of the SDR is loss the software cannot recover — no DSP

47
00:03:00,967 --> 00:03:03,303
setting refunds dB spent in the coax.

48
00:03:03,303 --> 00:03:08,417
In a link budget, every one of these losses is a negative term, and knowing where

49
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:13,594
the dB are going tells you where to spend effort: usually a shorter cable run, and

50
00:03:13,594 --> 00:03:15,109
a preamp at the antenna.

51
00:03:15,559 --> 00:03:21,141
So: attenuation is loss in decibels — spreading and absorption, adding up connector

52
00:03:21,141 --> 00:03:26,453
by connector — it hurts most before the first amplifier, and used deliberately,

53
00:03:26,453 --> 00:03:28,202
it's how you buy headroom.

54
00:03:28,202 --> 00:03:30,017
Full write-up linked below.

